Greetings modelers,

I am trying to fit a binomial n-mixture model in JAGS to a large data set collected between 2006 and 2018, but I’m stuck on a data formatting issue for which I can’t seem to find a solution. I should say I am very new to the Bayes world and so far self-taught, so I apologize for any naivety or silly questions.

These data are point counts conducted during the summer throughout the entire state of Iowa. I think Bayes is the way to go since it’s such a large data set and, in particular, the same sites are not always surveyed every year. During a year we randomly select up to 30 sites and, along with another 50 sites that are surveyed every year (i.e., "permanent sites"), visit the sites up to 4 times during the breeding period. These are standard 10-min. long point counts. Each site has 7 points that are visited during a morning – these are in a hexagonal shape, 200 m apart. I’ve simply collapsed these points to avoid dealing with spatial autocorrelation.

Basically, I want to estimate abundance and density for each year. I think an open-population model would be best but I’ve also just fit some models with year as a fixed effect. I’ve had some luck with fitting models based on code from the AHM book Vol 1 and Kery et al. (2009) but since every site is not surveyed every year and the number of sites changes each year, I have a lot of missing data if I use a multidimensional array.

My question is how can I format the data and the model to an open population model without using a multidimensional array? Below is some sample code for Field Sparrow from 2015 to 2018 only.

R = # sites over the entire 4 years (94)

T = # visits (3) – I’ve only include sites that were surveyed no more than 3 times (1 issue at a time...)

So this results in a 94x3x4 array. What I’d like to do is learn how to adapt this model where Year is a vector (I think) so that I have fewer missing data. I realize part of this is probably not being well-versed in this modelling language but any help would be much appreciated.

# ZIP model

cat("model{

    # Priors

    phi ~ dunif(0,1)

    for (k in 1:4){

      lam.int <http://lam.int>[k] ~ dnorm(0, 0.1) # lambda intercept

      p.int <http://p.int>[k] ~ dnorm(0, 0.1)  # p intercept

    }

    beta1~ dnorm(0, 0.1) # hab1 for abundance

    beta2 ~ dnorm(0, 0.1) # hab2 for abundance

    alpha1 ~ dnorm(0, 0.1) # cov1 for detection

    alpha2 ~ dnorm(0,0.1)  # cov2 for detection

    alpha3 ~ dnorm(0, 0.1)  # cov3 for detection

    # Likelihood

    # Ecological model for true abundance

    for (i in 1:R){                                      # Loop over R sites (95 for all years)

    z[i] ~ dbern(phi)

    for (k in 1:4){                              # Loop over years (4)

      N[i,k] ~ dpois(lam.eff[i,k])

      lam.eff[i,k] <- z[i] * lambda[i,k] + 0.00001

      log(lambda[i,k]) <- lam.int <http://lam.int>[k] + beta1*forb[i,,k] + beta2*shrub[i,,k]

    # Observation model for replicated counts

    for (j in 1:T){                           # Loop over temporal reps (2)

      y[i,j,k] ~ dbin(p[i,j,k], N[i,k])      # Detection

      p[i,j,k] <- exp(lp[i,j,k])/(1+exp(lp[i,j,k]))

      lp[i,j,k] <- p.int <http://p.int>[k] + alpha1*temp[i,j,k] + alpha2*wind[i,j,k] + alpha3*day[i,j,k]

    # Assess model fit using Chi-squared discrepancy

    # Compute fit statistic for observed data

    eval[i,j,k] <- p[i,j,k] * N[i,k]

    E[i,j,k] <- pow((y[i,j,k] - eval[i,j,k]),2) / (eval[i,j,k]+0.5)

    # Generate replicate data and compute fit stats for them

    y.new <http://y.new>[i,j,k] ~ dbin(p[i,j,k], N[i,k])

    E.new[i,j,k] <- pow((y.new <http://y.new>[i,j,k] - eval[i,j,k]),2) / (eval[i,j,k]+0.5)

    } #j

    ik.p[i,k] <- mean(p[i,,k])

    } #k

    } #i

    # Derived and other quantities

    for (k in 1:4){

    totalN[k] <- sum(N[,k])                 # Estimate total pop. size across all sites

    mean.abundance[k] <- mean(lambda[,k])   # Mean site abundance for each year

    mean.N[k] <- mean(N[,k])                # total abundance for each year

    mean.detection[k] <- mean(ik.p[,k])     # mean detection for each year

    }

    density2015 <- sum(N[,1])/(404*3.15*100*100*0.0001) # only used individuals detected within 100m to get density estimates

    density2016 <- sum(N[,2])/(501*3.15*100*100*0.0001)

    density2017 <- sum(N[,3])/(487*3.15*100*100*0.0001)

    density2018 <- sum(N[,4])/(331*3.15*100*100*0.0001)

    fit <- sum(E[,,])

    fit.new <http://fit.new> <- sum(E.new[,,])

