Hi Rachel,

It might be worth searching for ragged array on the list to see what other folks have done, but one way is to set up a data frame for the observations structured like:

>head(df)

y     site    year   visit
2     1         1        1
0     1         1        3
.....

The loop to define lambda, N, etc over sites and years and the loop for p can stay as is, but the loop for observations might look like:

for (o in 1:nobs){
y[o]~dbin(p[site[o], visit[o], year[o]], N[site[o], year[o]])
}

In the data list, make sure to define site as df$site, etc.

For what it's worth (or in case anybody else ends up poking around here), this will cut down on how much RAM JAGS (and also Nimble, *I think*) use up. I don't think there are many STAN users on here, but it works well for most purposes there, too (that program doesn't deal with padding NA's or empty cells well at all).

John

